│ Executive Summary                                                            │
│                                                                              │
│ The transition of artificial intelligence from static text synthesis to      │
│ autonomous decision-making hinges on building agents that can reason, plan,  │
│ and execute actions within dynamic environments. This review synthesizes     │
│ literature addressing the core question: what are the main approaches to     │
│ building AI agents that can reason and act?                                  │
│                                                                              │
│ The synthesized research reveals four primary architectural paradigms: (1)   │
│ synchronous interleaving of reasoning traces with external tool invocations  │
│ to ground action spaces; (2) deliberative tree-search planning that          │
│ overcomes the constraints of linear autoregressive generation via heuristic  │
│ exploration, lookahead, and backtracking; (3) non-parametric behavioral      │
│ adaptation driven by episodic verbal self-reflection and structured memory   │
│ streams; and (4) multi-agent conversational frameworks that distribute       │
│ complex problem-solving across specialized, interacting entities.            │
│                                                                              │
│ A central caveat emerges across this literature: while language model agents │
│ exhibit strong heuristic flexibility, their underlying reliance on           │
│ statistical pattern matching rather than first-principles world models       │
│ leaves them vulnerable to degenerative action loops, ungrounded              │
│ self-evaluations, and catastrophic planning failures when operating without  │
│ external formal verification.                                                │
│                                                                              │
│ Introduction                                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│ The development of artificial intelligence has historically aspired to       │
│ construct autonomous agents capable of perceiving external environments,     │
│ deliberating over sequential goals, and enacting changes to achieve desired  │
│ outcomes. With the advent of foundation models, the autonomous agent         │
│ paradigm has shifted from domain-specific heuristic systems toward flexible, │
│ language-driven architectures. Rather than treating neural models merely as  │
│ static knowledge bases or passive text generators, modern cognitive          │
│ frameworks position them as central decision-making controllers.             │
│                                                                              │
│ Understanding the mechanisms that facilitate autonomous agency is critical   │
│ as these systems are increasingly deployed to execute multi-step software    │
│ workflows, interact with digital application programming interfaces (APIs),  │
│ and simulate social dynamics. However, building agents that reliably         │
│ orchestrate internal cognition alongside external tool use presents          │
│ formidable challenges, including hallucinations, infinite execution loops,   │
│ and an absence of formal planning guarantees. This literature review         │
│ investigates the architectural frameworks designed to resolve these          │
│ challenges, addressing the specific research question: What are the main     │
│ approaches to building AI agents that can reason and act?                    │
│                                                                              │
│ Methodology                                                                  │
│                                                                              │
│ This review evaluates a curated corpus of 15 academic papers addressing      │
│ autonomous agent architectures, cognitive planning mechanisms, tool          │
│ integration, and multi-agent coordination. Evidence was identified and       │
│ extracted via semantic retrieval over segmented passage collections using    │
│ targeted keyword queries covering single-agent deliberation, tree-search     │
│ exploration, conversational programming, verbal reinforcement, and empirical │
│ planning limitations.                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│ The extracted evidence was subjected to thematic synthesis to identify       │
│ converging technical methodologies, conceptual frameworks, points of         │
│ empirical tension, and operational vulnerabilities. A methodological         │
│ limitation of this approach is its reliance on a predetermined, fixed        │
│ corpus. While this corpus captures seminal and representative frameworks in  │
│ foundation-model agency, it does not incorporate the entirety of an          │
│ exponentially expanding field, nor does it track real-time revisions to      │
│ empirical benchmarks or closed-source commercial implementations.            │
│ Consequently, the findings represent a structured qualitative synthesis of   │
│ core paradigms rather than an exhaustive meta-analysis.                      │
